WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.332 --> 00:00:03.499
(tapping)

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.710
- [Narrator] Hopefully you
know this already but ...

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:08.720
that's a toothbrush.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:09.820
So are these.

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:11.440
And the one thing they have in common:

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.600
they're all plastic.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.350
But here's something you might not know.

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:16.880
This routine has been
around for a millennia.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.677
And back then, they used chewing sticks.

00:00:19.677 --> 00:00:20.741
(sticks rattling)

00:00:20.741 --> 00:00:22.840
Fast forward a bit to 1498, China.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.256
They used a bamboo
handle and some hog hair.

00:00:25.256 --> 00:00:26.280
(snorting)

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:27.780
But here's the thing.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.140
One estimate says one billion toothbrushes

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:33.180
are ending up here every year in the U.S.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.680
And that sounds bad.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.607
But we might be able to change that.

00:00:36.607 --> 00:00:39.883
(upbeat music)

00:00:39.883 --> 00:00:42.466
(gulls cawing)

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:45.728
Now, let's go meet a dentist.

00:00:45.728 --> 00:00:48.811
(bouncy piano music)

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:53.340
- I'm Dr. Scott Swank, dentist.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:55.840
I've been practicing for 30 years.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.400
- [Narrator] But Scott
isn't just any dentist.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.870
- I'm currently the curator
at the National Museum

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:02.670
of Dentistry in Baltimore, Maryland.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.890
This is George Washington's lower denture.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:11.310
And these are dental
scalers fit for a queen.

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:14.400
These were actually
owned by Queen Victoria.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.863
This is the Swiss army knife
of tooth extraction devices.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:25.670
Brushing your teeth became
important during the Civil War.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:27.930
Men were being disqualified for service

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:31.770
because they didn't have
two opposing back teeth,

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.720
and they needed those
in order to tear open

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.105
paper rifle and musket cartridges.

00:01:37.105 --> 00:01:40.105
(emphatic drumming)

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.600
- [Narrator] So, here's
what that looks like.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:47.370
- [Reenactor] Fire.
(rifle firing)

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:48.460
- [Narrator] And that's pretty hard to do

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:50.280
if you don't have, um ...

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:51.560
teeth.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:53.460
But here was the problem with that.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.500
Toothbrushes hadn't really
caught on yet in America.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:00.460
By 1924, only about 20% of
Americans brushed their teeth,

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:02.450
which is gross.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:05.340
And then the Dupont
Company introduced nylon.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.940
to the American public in the late 1930s.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:12.940
And, boom, the very first
nylon bristle toothbrush.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.355
Then came World War II.

00:02:15.355 --> 00:02:19.200
- But most important of
all, clean your teeth often.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.327
Clean them well.

00:02:21.327 --> 00:02:23.240
- [Narrator] And it was
during the war that the Army

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:25.780
decided to give every soldier a toothbrush

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:29.320
and make it a mandatory
part of their daily routine.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:30.670
And that toothbrush design,

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:33.903
it hasn't really changed since.

00:02:43.342 --> 00:02:45.460
(staccato electronic music)

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.010
This is Charlotte Fiell.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:51.090
- F-I-E-L-L, and it's pronounced Fiell.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:52.640
- [Narrator] She's a
leading expert on design

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:54.630
and has written a lot of books,

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.030
including this one on
the history of plastics.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:58.570
- It was a fascinating journey,

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:02.860
because I didn't realize what
a incredible history it had.

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:06.660
In the future, we have to use
plastics more thoughtfully.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:07.890
When you actually think about them,

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:11.250
they're incredibly noble,
precious materials.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:15.150
There's no reason why something
that's made of plastic

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:17.449
can't last a lifetime.

00:03:17.449 --> 00:03:20.190
(bright electronic music)

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:22.680
- [Narrator] The answer:
three to four months,

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.180
at least according to the ADA.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.630
So with 300 million Americans,

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:29.210
that's 1.2 billion toothbrushes

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:31.410
being thrown away just in America.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.640
That's enough to warp
around the world four times.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.480
Now, let's look ahead.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.930
- If you're a designer,
you have an absolute duty

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:41.430
to design properly, especially,

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:43.800
especially if they're using plastics,

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.670
because plastics might be cheap materials,

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:47.860
but they actually have

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:51.830
a very high impact on the environment.

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:53.950
They're very expensive materials

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:57.130
when you actually think
of them in a holistic way.

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:59.000
- [Narrator] Okay, so
here are some options.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.620
We could use alternative materials:

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:03.660
bone, metal, recycled
plastic, or bamboo could work.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:06.040
There are toothbrushes
with replaceable heads,

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:07.720
and then there's pig
hair, which biodegrades,

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:09.570
but it wouldn't be an option for vegans

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:11.870
and people with certain religious beliefs.

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:14.290
Lastly, chewing sticks
created from the neem tree

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:16.450
are a totally plastic-free option.

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:18.820
But that would be a huge cultural shift.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.760
Change takes time.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.970
And because we've been using
the same toothbrush design

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:27.040
for about 70 years, maybe that time is ...

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:28.009
now.

00:04:28.009 --> 00:04:31.676
(blissful electronic music)

